---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Why Scam Drills Work: The Research Behind ScamPrep"
  description="Federal research shows scam classes fade in a month, while simulated experience sticks. The science and the proven enterprise playbook behind ScamPrep."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="kicker">Why it works</p>
      <h1>What the research says about scam training</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        ScamPrep applies two decades of behavioral research and a playbook proven across
        industry to fortify defenses against scams.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The argument as an evidence chain: numbered amber nodes joined by a
       vertical trace line. Steps rise in on scroll (markup ships the final
       state; no-JS and reduced-motion visitors see everything, unanimated). -->
  <section class="section--tight section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <ol class="case" id="case">
        <li class="case-step">
          <span class="case-step__num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          <div class="case-step__body">
            <h2>One-time education wears off</h2>
            <p>
              In 2024, a federal advisory group reviewed the research on scam-prevention
              messaging. The findings were blunt: benefits of a scam-awareness class
              <em>largely fade within about a month</em>. Attendees of in-person tutorials
              performed no better than anyone else six months later, and retention persisted
              only when reminders kept coming. People blindly click past in-the-moment pop-up
              warnings. One-and-done education is the standard response to elder fraud, and it
              demonstrably doesn't work.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="case-step">
          <span class="case-step__num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
          <div class="case-step__body">
            <h2>Safe experience is what changes behavior</h2>
            <p>
              The same review found the most promising approach is letting people safely
              experience a scam, that means delivering training messages when users click on
              links in fake phishing messages. This is because of what psychologists call
              <em>optimism bias</em>, that sense of &ldquo;it won't happen to me.&rdquo;
              Statistics and education don't break it, but a near-miss might. A University of
              Florida study sent simulated phishing emails to real inboxes over 30 days and
              found susceptibility was measurable, trainable, and predictive of real-world
              behavior. That's the ScamPrep model: repetitive safe experiences to immediate
              coaching, which together lead to measurable resilience.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="case-step">
          <span class="case-step__num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          <div class="case-step__body">
            <h2>The playbook is proven at massive scale</h2>
            <p>
              Virtually every large company trains employees with simulated phishing: fake the
              attack on company systems and coach whomever clicks. It's a multibillion-dollar
              industry because it works. But this approach has <em>only ever been pointed at
              office workers</em>. The demographic losing the most money to these scams has
              never had a version built for them, until ScamPrep.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Ground-up design based on the research</h2>
      <p>
        The same research that validates simulation is specific about how to do it right with
        older adults: positive framing, never fear or shame; immediate, gentle debriefs; consent
        and trust as the foundation. We built those insights into the product's bones.
        <a href="/promise">Read our promise</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="sources">
        <li>
          FTC Scams Against Older Adults Advisory Group,
          <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/system/files/consumer_ftc_gov/pdf/A%20Review%20of%20Scam%20Prevention%20Messaging%20Research.pdf"><em>A Review of Scam Prevention Messaging Research</em> (2024)</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          University of Florida,
          <a href="https://academic.oup.com/pnasnexus/article/3/8/pgae296/7725152">simulated-phishing study in older adults, <em>PNAS Nexus</em> (2024)</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center,
          <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2025_IC3Report.pdf">2025 Annual Report</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Cybersecurity Ventures,
          <a href="https://cybersecurityventures.com/security-awareness-training-market-to-hit-10-billion-annually-by-2027">security-awareness training market analysis</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    headline="Want this for your family?"
    body="Join the early-access list and give your family the version of this training that offices have had for a decade."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  /* ---------- Evidence chain ---------- */
  .case {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .case-step {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 44px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0 22px;
    padding-bottom: 44px;
  }
  .case-step:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }
  /* The connecting trace between nodes. */
  .case-step::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 21px;
    top: 52px;
    bottom: 6px;
    width: 1.5px;
    background: var(--line-strong, var(--line));
  }
  .case-step:last-child::before { display: none; }
  .case-step__num {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    background: var(--amber-tint);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--amber);
    color: var(--amber-deep);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .case-step__body h2 { margin: 8px 0 10px; font-size: 1.45rem; }
  .case-step__body p { margin: 0; }

  /* Scroll-in: node lands with a one-time ping, body rises after — the same
     grammar as the Resilience Report card. Classes are armed by JS only when
     motion is allowed, so the hidden states never apply without JS. */
  .case-step--anim .case-step__num,
  .case-step--anim .case-step__body { opacity: 0; }
  .case-step.is-in .case-step__num {
    animation: caseRise 0.45s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1) forwards;
  }
  .case-step.is-in .case-step__body {
    animation: caseRise 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1) 0.12s forwards;
  }
  .case-step.is-in .case-step__num::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: -14px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1.5px solid rgba(240, 161, 28, 0.8);
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    animation: casePing 0.9s cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1) 0.25s forwards;
  }
  @keyframes caseRise {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
  @keyframes casePing {
    from { transform: scale(0.55); opacity: 0.9; }
    to { transform: scale(1); opacity: 0; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .case-step { grid-template-columns: 36px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 16px; }
    .case-step__num { width: 36px; height: 36px; font-size: 0.95rem; }
    .case-step::before { left: 17px; top: 44px; }
  }

  .sources { padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .sources li { margin-bottom: 0.6em; }
</style>

<script>
  // Arm the evidence-chain reveal only when motion is allowed; play each step
  // once as it scrolls into view.
  const steps = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.case-step'));
  const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
  if (steps.length && 'IntersectionObserver' in window && !reduce) {
    steps.forEach((s) => s.classList.add('case-step--anim'));
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            entry.target.classList.add('is-in');
            io.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        });
      },
      { threshold: 0.25 }
    );
    steps.forEach((s) => io.observe(s));
  }
</script>
